---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SocialShare from "../../components/SocialShare.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="How to Calculate Zakat on Gold (2026 Guide)"
    description="Learn how to calculate Zakat on gold. Covers Nisab, carat conversion, current prices, and a free online calculator for Pakistan."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <header class="mb-8">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">
                Published: October 20, 2026
            </p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                How to Calculate Zakat on Gold (2026 Guide)
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                Gold is one of the most common assets subject to Zakat — but
                calculating it correctly involves Nisab, carat conversion, and
                current market prices. Here's exactly how to do it.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                Zakat on gold is one of the most frequently asked questions in
                Islamic finance. Gold holds value, appreciates over time, and is
                often given as gifts — which means most Muslim families have
                some gold stored somewhere, and many are unsure how to calculate
                Zakat on it.
            </p>
            <p>
                The rules are clear but involve a few moving parts: the Nisab
                threshold (in grams), the carat of your gold, the current market
                price, and the 2.5% rate. This guide walks through each step
                with real examples.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Quick Answer: Zakat on Gold
            </h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Nisab:</strong> 87.48 grams (7.5 tola) of pure gold
                </li>
                <li><strong>Rate:</strong> 2.5% of the total market value</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Rate applies</strong> after holding gold for one lunar
                    year (Hawl)
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Calculate:</strong> (Gold in grams × current price per
                    gram) × 2.5%
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Use our <a
                    href="/tools/zakat-calculator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                    >free Zakat Calculator</a
                > to do all of this instantly.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Step 1: Understand Nisab
            </h2>
            <p>
                Nisab is the minimum amount of gold you must own before Zakat
                becomes obligatory. For gold, the Nisab is <strong
                    >87.48 grams</strong
                > — equal to 7.5 tola.
            </p>
            <p>
                If your total gold (across all jewelry, coins, and bars) is less
                than 87.48 grams, you don't owe Zakat on gold. If it's above
                that threshold and you've held it for one lunar year, Zakat
                becomes due.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Note:</strong> Some scholars use silver Nisab (612.36 grams
                of silver) instead of gold, because silver Nisab is lower — which
                means more people qualify. The choice is yours, but most Islamic scholars
                in the Hanafi school recommend the silver Nisab for safety.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Step 2: Convert Your Gold to Pure (24K) Weight
            </h2>
            <p>
                Most gold jewelry in Pakistan is not pure 24K — it's usually 21K
                or 22K. To calculate Zakat correctly, you need to convert your
                gold to its equivalent weight in pure 24K gold.
            </p>
            <p>Use this formula:</p>
            <p
                class="text-gray-700 p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg font-mono text-sm"
            >
                Pure gold weight = Total grams × (Carat ÷ 24)
            </p>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Carat</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Conversion Factor</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Example: 100g becomes</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">24K (pure)</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">÷ 1</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">100g pure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">22K</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">× (22 ÷ 24) = 0.9167</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">91.67g pure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">21K</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">× (21 ÷ 24) = 0.875</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">87.5g pure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">18K</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">× (18 ÷ 24) = 0.75</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">75g pure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">14K</td>
                            <td class="p-3">× (14 ÷ 24) = 0.5833</td>
                            <td class="p-3">58.33g pure</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Step 3: Check the Current Gold Price
            </h2>
            <p>
                Zakat is calculated on the current market value of your gold,
                not the price you paid when you bought it.
            </p>
            <p>
                Look up today's gold rate per gram for 24K gold. In Pakistan,
                you can find this at <a
                    href="https://gold.pk/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium">gold.pk</a
                > or your local sarafa bazaar.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Important:</strong> Use the 24K price, not the 22K or 21K
                price. If you're converting your gold to pure weight (Step 2), you
                should use the pure gold price for consistency.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Step 4: Calculate the Zakat
            </h2>
            <p>Now apply the formula:</p>
            <p
                class="text-gray-700 p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg font-mono text-sm"
            >
                Zakat = Pure gold weight (grams) × Current gold price per gram ×
                2.5%
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">A Worked Example</h2>
            <p>Suppose you have the following:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li><strong>Gold jewelry:</strong> 150 grams of 22K gold</li>
                <li><strong>Gold coins:</strong> 30 grams of 24K gold</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Current 24K gold price:</strong> PKR 22,000 per gram
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Step 1: Convert to pure gold</strong><br />
                Jewelry: 150 × (22 ÷ 24) = 137.5 grams pure<br />
                Coins: 30 × 1 = 30 grams pure<br />
                <strong>Total pure gold:</strong> 167.5 grams
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Step 2: Check Nisab</strong><br />
                167.5 grams > 87.48 grams → <strong>Nisab met</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Step 3: Calculate value</strong><br />
                167.5 × 22,000 = <strong>PKR 3,685,000</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Step 4: Calculate Zakat</strong><br />
                3,685,000 × 0.025 = <strong>PKR 92,125</strong>
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4">
                That's the total Zakat due on your gold for the year.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                What About Jewelry in Regular Use?
            </h2>
            <p>
                This is one of the most debated topics in Islamic finance. The
                four major schools of thought differ:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Hanafi school:</strong> All gold jewelry is Zakatable,
                    whether worn regularly or stored.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Shafi'i, Maliki, Hanbali schools:</strong> Jewelry in
                    regular use is not Zakatable.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                For safety and to be on the conservative side, most scholars in
                the Hanafi tradition — which is common in Pakistan — recommend
                including all gold jewelry in your Zakat calculation. If you
                follow a different school, consult your scholar.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Do I Include Gold I Plan to Sell?
            </h2>
            <p>
                Yes. Any gold you own — whether stored, worn, or set aside for
                future sale — is Zakatable. The only exception is gold that is
                actively being used as a tool (which is rare).
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Do I Include Gold I've Given as Mahr?
            </h2>
            <p>
                Gold given as Mahr (dowry) that has been transferred to the
                bride's ownership is hers to calculate Zakat on. It's Zakatable
                by the bride, not the giver.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Do I Include Gold I've Bought But Not Received?
            </h2>
            <p>
                Gold you've paid for but not yet received is not in your
                possession. It becomes Zakatable when you take delivery — from
                that point, if you hold it for a full lunar year and meet the
                Nisab, it's Zakatable.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 mt-3">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        How much gold do I need to pay Zakat?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        The Nisab threshold is 87.48 grams of pure (24K) gold.
                        If your total gold is above this and you've held it for
                        one lunar year, Zakat is due.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Do I calculate Zakat on my daily-wear jewelry?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        According to the Hanafi school, yes. Some other schools
                        exempt daily-wear jewelry. Consult your scholar if
                        you're unsure.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What if I have gold in a different carat than 24K?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Convert it to pure gold weight first: pure gold weight =
                        total grams × (carat ÷ 24). Then apply the Zakat rate.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Do I use the price I paid or the current price?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Zakat is calculated on the current market value of your
                        gold, not the original purchase price.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Is Zakat on gold paid annually?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes. Zakat is due once per lunar year (Hawl) on gold
                        you've held that entire year.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Can I use your calculator to do this?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes. Our <a
                            href="/tools/zakat-calculator/"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >free Zakat Calculator</a
                        > handles all of this — carat conversion, Nisab checks, and
                        Zakat calculation — in one place.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p>
                Calculating Zakat on gold is simple once you know the three
                inputs: carat, weight, and current price. Convert to pure gold
                weight first, then apply the 2.5% rate.
            </p>
            <p>
                For the fastest calculation, use our <a
                    href="/tools/zakat-calculator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                    >free Zakat Calculator</a
                > — it handles carat conversion, Nisab checks, and total Zakat in
